WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.700
I wanted to make pieces that
seem as if they were moving,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.700
or living as if they
were really breathing.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.180
My work, it's so many things.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.750
It's joyful, it's pop,

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.253
it's craft, it's Baroque, it's decadent.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.323
This is King style.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.323
My name is King Houndekpinkou
and I'm a ceramic artist.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.333
Paris is my base, it's the lab.

00:01:04.139 --> 00:01:06.960
There is a long wall in my studio

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.210
that has a lot of images on it,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.870
and these images represent
things that influence me,

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.970
or bits of the history of ceramics,

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.590
or the music that I like,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.260
or the type of pieces
that I like and so on.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.140
And if you had to print out my brain,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.343
you would get that wall.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.213
That's where I can just
write whatever I feel.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.213
It's as confusing as my brain works.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.860
As soon as I have an idea, I can draw it,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.910
and then I'll look at the drawings,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.040
play with the colors,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.070
and next step is choosing
the right type of clay

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.383
to make the type of work that I wanna do.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.250
Then I wedge the clay.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.286
That enables you to take
off all the air bubbles

00:02:05.286 --> 00:02:07.985
in the clay and prepare the clay.

00:02:07.985 --> 00:02:10.530
And then once the clay is well-prepared

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.530
and ready to be worked
on I get on the wheel.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.750
And that's when I start
throwing the basic shapes

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.003
that will make the base of my artwork.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.860
To make a piece it might take me

00:02:37.485 --> 00:02:40.800
three weeks to two months roughly,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.800
but it's more complicated than that

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.140
because I work on so
many pieces at the time.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.843
I work on 20 pieces at the time.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.410
So while some pieces are drying,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.030
I'm working on another piece

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:05.030
and then while some glazes are
drying, I'm on another piece,

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.703
so it's never one piece.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.190
In 2012 I went to visit a friend in Tokyo

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.960
and we went out one night,

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.263
and I met this Japanese girl
who told me about pottery.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.070
And at the time also, I was
sort of, like soul-searching.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.700
Like I was already involved
in a career in communications

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.700
but I knew that my creativity
could serve better causes.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.540
And she told me about it,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.050
and somehow that word
pottery stayed in my mind,

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.080
and it kept coming back,
coming back, coming back.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.120
And when I came back to Paris

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.480
I found this Japanese pottery workshop

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.913
where I could start
pottery for the first time.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.440
And once I touched the clay,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.050
it's almost as if, like,
sleeping thoughts awakened

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.603
and that's when things changed for me.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:20.690
It's almost as if you feel
like there's sort of like

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.410
electricity coming from your hands

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.550
throughout your whole body,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:30.570
and it awakens something, that seed inside

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.570
and then you question
your whole life again.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:47.810
I trained between Paris
and Bizen in Japan.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:52.060
Bizen is one of the six ancestral

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.268
cities for pottery in Japan.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.420
There I was able to connect
with Toshiaki Shibuta

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.733
who became my sort of,
like, pottery father.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.080
Him and his friends taught
me the Kikuneri technique,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.883
the spiral wedging technique.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.290
It's sort of, like, prints
in the clay's memory

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.053
the circular movements

00:05:27.053 --> 00:05:29.973
and so it's easier for you to throw.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.064
He taught me so many things,

00:05:36.064 --> 00:05:39.872
for example, throwing slowly on the wheel

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:43.230
and taking your time while you throw.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:44.853
You don't have to throw fast

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:50.580
and let the clay absorb
each of your movements,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:54.663
and be slow with it, be gentle with it.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.730
My friends in Japan
taught me so many things

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.140
about their culture first of all.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:04.230
But also about life

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.020
because I lost my dad very early on,

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.260
like, when I was eight, nine.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:15.260
So somehow through ceramics
I sort of, like, found

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:21.063
other fathers and big brothers
that could teach me things.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:25.950
We are very different.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.073
It's a different generation,
different lifestyle,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.923
different skin color,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:36.150
but we connect thanks to the clay,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:41.013
and for me that type of
relationship is gold.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.853
At the beginning I was only
making functional pieces.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:55.070
And because I looked up to
my Japanese pottery fathers

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.640
I wanted to be like them,

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.700
but they also made me realize that no,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.990
I cannot be like them and I should be me.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.940
That's when I started to
make more sculptural pieces

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.490
and realized that,

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.600
yes, my energy wasn't a potter energy,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.300
it was more of an artist's energy,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.390
someone that wants to
create different things

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.843
and not make the same
thing over and over again.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.490
When I was younger I used
to be bullied, like, a lot,

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:39.930
and was sort of, like, not
accepted by my other peers.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:44.580
And I was able to find,

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.000
like, security and peace in
video games and virtual world

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.380
where I could control the character,

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:54.360
where I could choose the
character I wanna be,

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.103
and go on an adventure.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:03.440
Games like Zelda or Metal Gear Solid.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:10.650
It's so amazing to be able
to pull these concepts

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:14.370
and grow these concepts
that you grew up with,

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:15.570
that you had in your childhood

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.990
and, like, drag them with
you into your adulthood

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.600
and bring a message out of it.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.600
I then was able to dig deeper in who I am,

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.220
where I come from, my roots

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:35.943
from West Africa in Benin with
the spirituality and so on.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.950
When I trained in Bizen
I sensed the spirituality

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.833
in the way that my friends
were making their works,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.182
and that spirituality is highly infused

00:08:49.182 --> 00:08:51.540
by the culture of Shinto.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.270
And that made me realize

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.060
that that spirituality was very similar

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.090
to the one of voodoo in
Benin in West Africa,

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:04.140
and both religion or system of beliefs,

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.480
they share the roots of animism.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.480
And there was this, I don't
know, like, it resonated in me.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:17.649
It also feeds me as a maker

00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:19.830
in my studio when I create my work

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.500
because I use clays from
different countries,

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:23.700
from both countries too.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.820
I don't have an academic
background in arts,

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.820
so I just use my intuition
to put colors together,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:40.850
but I have sort of, like,
developed my own theory about

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.810
the way I use color and one of them,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.810
one of the theories is that
for my work it's 80% texture

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:56.043
and 20% pigment

00:09:57.109 --> 00:10:00.090
and that's how I work.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:05.090
So what really brings life
and gives life to the color,

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.473
or the piece really is the texture.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.711
Working with the four elements:

00:10:16.711 --> 00:10:19.286
earth, fire, air and water

00:10:19.286 --> 00:10:22.140
is amazing because these elements

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.840
are older than us human beings.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.490
And I truly believe that these elements

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:31.470
know more than we do.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.770
It's almost as if I'm asking
questions about my future

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:38.643
and I'm consulting the clay
to gain knowledge out of it.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:47.760
I've had people asking
me if it tears my heart

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.920
to part from a work,

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.270
or just sell a work and see
a piece leave the studio,

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:55.560
and I'm like, no.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.810
Like the whole adventure
of thinking of the piece,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.550
and then making it,

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.466
and having it out there

00:11:02.466 --> 00:11:06.090
is the whole fun for me.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:09.330
Then what comes at the end of that process

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.793
is a byproduct of that whole adventure.

